<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jainism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jain religion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jainism is an Indian religion that teaches liberation of the soul through non-violence, truthfulness, non-attachment and strict self-discipline. Its followers revere 24 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">tirthankaras</a>, the last of whom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, lived in the 6th or 5th century BCE. Divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> and Shvetambara traditions, Jains have long been prominent as merchants and patrons of temples, such as those of Palitana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jainism.jpg" alt="Jainism" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthew Logelin · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shravanbelgola_Gomateshvara_feet_prayer1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Jainism is an ancient Indian religion rooted in the independent Śramaṇa tradition of ascetics, which developed alongside but distinct from the orthodox Vedic religion. Jains maintain that their faith is eternal and that in the present cosmic time cycle it has been guided by 24 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">tirthankaras</a>, beginning with Rishabhanatha. Historians date the 23rd, Pārśvanātha, to about the eighth or seventh century BCE. The 24th, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, was a contemporary of the Buddha and a central figure in the Śramaṇa movement of Greater Magadha, which rejected the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>.</p><p>Some scholars have proposed that seals from the Indus Valley Civilization show early forms of Jain asceticism, but because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-script">Indus script</a> is undeciphered, mainstream historians treat this as a hypothesis. The Hathigumpha Inscription of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharavela">Kharavela</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> at the Udayagiri Caves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, from the second century BCE, records his patronage of Jain monks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Jainism teaches a path to spiritual purity through disciplined nonviolence, ahimsa, to all living creatures. It is transtheistic: the universe was not created and has no creator, governor, judge or destroyer. Its philosophy rests on three ethical pillars, ahimsa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anekantavada">anekantavada</a>, the doctrine that reality has many sides and no single viewpoint is completely true, and aparigraha, non-possession. Purification of the soul and liberation, moksha, are held to be achievable through the three jewels of right vision, right knowledge and right conduct. Laypersons keep five small vows and mendicants five great vows. Nonviolence led the community to avoid agriculture and warfare, and many Jains became merchants and bankers.</p><p>Devout Jains practise lacto-vegetarianism, and monks and nuns and some followers avoid root vegetables, since tiny organisms are injured when the plant is pulled up. The most fundamental invocation, accepted by all sects, is the Namokar Mantra. The most important annual festival is Paryushana among the Śvētāmbaras and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dasa">Dasa</a> lakshana parva among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambaras</a>, a time of fasting and prayer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The community is divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> (sky-clad) and Śvētāmbara (white-clad) traditions, which differ on monastic discipline, canonical texts and the liberation of women. The Digambara account places the origin of the schism around the fourth century BCE, when Bhadrabahu predicted a twelve-year famine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> and led monks to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. Most modern scholars, such as Padmanabh Jaini and Paul Dundas, conclude that it was a gradual hardening of differences over several centuries.</p><p>Royal patronage helped Jainism grow and decline. Rashtrakuta kings sponsored major Jain cave temples, and the Yadava dynasty built many temples at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellora-caves">Ellora Caves</a> between 700 and 1000 CE. The Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendravarman-i">Mahendravarman I</a> converted from Jainism to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>. Virchand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, a Gujarati Jain scholar, represented Jainism at the first World Parliament of Religions in 1893 in Chicago. Jainism has between four and five million followers, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jainism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>British India was a predominantly rural society of peasant cultivators, organised by caste, religion and region, with Hindus forming the majority and Muslims the largest minority, alongside Sikhs, Christians, <b>Jains</b>, Parsis and tribal peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings presented themselves as patrons of Vedic sacrifice and of the gods Vishnu and Shiva, yet they also supported <b>Jainism</b>: the Meguti temple at Aihole, which carries the inscription of 634, is a Jain shrine, and one of the four cave temples at Badami is Jain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cholas were followers of Hinduism and were not swayed by Buddhism and <b>Jainism</b> as the Pallava and Pandya kings had been.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The early Hoysalas patronised <b>Jainism</b>, and Vishnuvardhana’s queen Shantala Devi and his general Gangaraja endowed Jain shrines at Shravanabelagola.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, attained enlightenment at Bodh Gaya in Magadha and preached at Rajagriha, where Bimbisara gave his order the Veluvana bamboo grove; Vardhamana Mahavira, the great teacher of <b>Jainism</b>, also taught in the region, and the Ajivikas of Makkhali Gosala were active there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Society was predominantly Hindu, with the Wodeyars patrons of Srivaishnava and Shaiva temples, including the Ranganatha temple at Srirangapatna and the Chamundeshwari temple on the hill above Mysore, while <b>Jains</b> and a growing Muslim population formed important communities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
